{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.adrian.edu/files/assets/accountingadjunctgraduatelevel-spring2024.pdf": {
      "title": "Accounting Adjunct",
      "description": "Accounting Adjunct.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \nPosition Title: Adjunct Instructor of Accounting, Department of Business, Accounting and \nEconomics.  \n   Part-time; up to 3 credit hours during the Spring 2024 Semester \n \nResponsibilities:   \nThis position involves frequent verbal, written and email correspondence with students and department faculty \nmembers. The successful candidate will: \n \n \n Instruct (In person) graduate course in ACCT 523: Individual Compliance and Planning. \n Administer exams and assign work outside the classroom \n Report mid-term and final exam grades  within College-specified timelines \n Maintain regular availability to support students \n \nMinimum requirements: \n Master’s Degree required in Accounting or a related field. \n Demonstrated ability to foster positive interactions with diverse populations. \n Must be able to perform duties within the normal bounds of a classroom environment.  \n Ability to offer the course online. \n \nCourse Description:  \nACCT 523: This course covers individual tax planning strategies, basics of mergers and acquisitions, asset \nacquisition, use and disposition, tax accounting methods, trusts, tax-exempt organizations, and tax research \ntechniques. \n \nDesired Qualifications:   \n Experience teaching at the college level \n Proficiency in software such as BlackBoard \n \nInterested individuals should submit a CV, unofficial transcripts, and contact information for three \nreferences to Tony Coumoundouros (acoumoundouros@adrian.edu). \n \n"
    },
    "https://www.adrian.edu/files/assets/adjunctbiologypositionfall2025.pdf": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor of Biology",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Biology.  \n \nTitle: Adjunct Instructor of Biology \nDepartment:  Department of Biology \n \nDescription: \nThe Adrian College Biology Department invites applications for adjunct instructor position with responsibilities to teach \nstarting in the beginning of January (2025). Specific teaching responsibilities will include Microbiology and labs. Twenty \nstudents are currently enrolled in lecture for M, W, and F at 1:00-1:50pm and 2 lab sections on T from 10:00-11:50am and \n1:00- 2:50pm.  Adrian College is a small liberal arts college with faculty opportunities to engage in research projects with \nstudents. The successful candidate should be able to develop microbiology-related field and lab projects with students.  \n \nMinimum Qualifications:   \n• A PhD in a Biological field is preferred. Highly qualified candidates who have a Master’s degree or are all but  \ndissertation will be considered. \n \nDesired Qualifications:  \n• Preference will be given to candidates with previous higher education teaching experience and experience in the \nfield. \n• Strong verbal and written communication skills and strong time management and organizational skills. \n \nTo apply, please submit a cover letter outlining your background and interests in teaching undergraduates, CV, \nundergraduate and graduate transcripts (must have official transcripts for final consideration), completed job \nemployment application (found on the Adrian College website), and the names and contact information (including \nphone numbers and emails) of three references to bpawlisch@adrian.edu. \n \nApplications will be accepted and reviewed until the position is filled \n \nAdrian College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. If offered a position, finalist must furnish proof of U.S. \ncitizenship or proof of eligibility to work in the U.S. within three (3) days of commencing employment.  \n \nA private, co-educational college of liberal arts and sciences related to the United Methodist Church, Adrian College is a \ntraditional four-year residential institution offering a focused undergraduate and graduate education. The College offers a \nmultitude of majors and pre-professional programs including certifications, approximately 50 athletic teams and 10 \ninstitutes dedicated to a diverse and personalized approach to education. Adrian College features state-of-the-art facilities, \nsmall class sizes and an innovative approach to the academic and personal growth of its students.  \n \nU.S. News & World Report has recognized and ranked Adrian College as a ‘Best Regional Colleges for Undergraduate \nTeaching’ and ‘Most Innovative’. Adrian also received accolades as a “Best Value” school. The College landed on the list \nof Best Value schools in the Midwest region for the sixth consecutive year and has also been recognized as having ‘A \nFocus on Student Success’; a new section in college rankings. Adrian College is located in Southeast Michigan, about 45 \nminutes from Ann Arbor and Toledo. \n"
    },
    "https://www.adrian.edu/files/assets/adjunctgconcoursesfall2025.pdf": {
      "title": "Great Conversation Adjunct",
      "description": "Great Conversation Adjunct.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \nPosition Title: Adjunct Professor  \nTeaching Load: Part-time; up to 9 credit hours during the Fall 2025 Semester \n \nContext: \nGeneral Education in the Liberal Arts: Great Conversation Courses \nAdrian College’s Essential Liberal Arts curriculum includes five great conversation courses: The Meaning of \nLife, Western Civilization, Global Civilizations, Art, and What It Means To Be A U.S. Citizen.  These courses \nare inspired by the Great Books pedagogy and use readings from the great texts and other methods to ensure \nthat every Adrian College student will attain the minimum historical and cultural literacy required of a college \ngraduate. The objective of each course is to present students with “the best that has been thought and said” \nas Matthew Arnold put it. They are designed to stimulate intellectual curiosity and more advanced study. \nMoreover, grouping these courses together as a series will allow give students a more connected and coherent \nacademic experience. They will study the same or similar material, themes, and texts as students in other \nsections. This will encourage students across campus to discuss commonly considered topics and ideas and \nconnect them as they move from course to course. \n \nAdrian College is seeking instructors for the following courses: \n \n GCON 120: Western Civilization. \n Course Description: This is a survey course that explores the rise, fall, and unique identities of \nnations and peoples descended from ancient Greece and the Roman Empire, especially \nEuropean nations, their colonies, and the cultures that emerged from them. This course entails \na writing component. \n \n GCON 130: Global Civilizations. \n Course Description: This is a survey course that explores the history and culture of Asian, \nAfrican, Native American, and other global nations and peoples. This course entails a writing \ncomponent.  \n \n GCON 140: Art. \n Course Description: The course combines analyses of renowned artworks with immersive \nexperiences, and may include visits to local museums, galleries, and live performances. \nEmphasis is placed on cultivating critical thinking skills to deeply analyze and appreciate the \nnuances of artistic expression. This course has a writing component. \n \n \nResponsibilities:   \nThis position involves teaching 3-credit courses in-person and will require frequent in-person and written \ninteractions with students. The instructor will:  \n \n Instruct (In person) undergraduate courses which include required writing and speaking assignments. \n Hold in-person office hours as well as providing electronic support for students outside class meeting \ntimes \n Report mid-term and final grades within college-specified timelines \n Follow common syllabus requirements, including posting assignment and course grades in Blackboard. \n\n \nMinimum requirements: \n Master’s Degree in areas in the Humanities related to these courses. \n Demonstrated ability to foster positive interactions with diverse populations \n Must be able to perform duties within the normal bounds of a college classroom environment \n \nDesired Qualifications:   \n Experience teaching in these areas at the college level \n Proficiency using course management software such as BlackBoard \n \n \nInterested individuals should contact Dr. Tony Coumoundouros at acoumoundouros@adrian.edu.  They \nmust send and CV and indicate which course or courses they would like to teach. \n \nApplications will be accepted and reviewed until the position is filled \n \nAdrian College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. If offered a position, finalist must furnish proof \nof U.S. citizenship or proof of eligibility to work in the U.S. within three (3) days of commencing employment. "
    },
    "https://www.adrian.edu/files/assets/adjunctprofessorofphysicsjobdescription.pdf": {
      "title": "Physics Adjunct",
      "description": "Physics Adjunct. Adjunct Professor in Physics \n \nThe Department of Physics at Adrian College invites applications for an adjunct professor \nposition for the Spring 2027 semester. \n \nSummary \n \nThe successful applicant will teach one section of Physics 104 – Introductory Physics II Lab \n(Wednesdays, 4:00-6:50pm) and two sections of Physics 203 – Mechanics Laboratory \n(Thursdays, 9:00-11:50am & 1:00-3:50pm). All sections will be taught in-person at Adrian \nCollege. \n \nJob Description \n \nDuties include, but are not limited to: \n• Leading scheduled lab sessions using the department's existing lab activities and \nmanual, including a brief introduction to the physics and procedure at the start of \neach session \n• Working directly with student groups during lab to guide data collection and \nanalysis \n• Grading lab reports and other lab work, returning feedback in a timely manner, and \nsubmitting �inal grades by college deadlines \n• Setting up and troubleshooting equipment in coordination with department staff, \nand reporting damaged or missing equipment \n• Maintaining a safe lab environment and enforcing lab safety procedures \n• Responding promptly to student email and coordinating with the department on \nattendance and course policies \n \nQuali�ications \n \nMinimum quali�ications are an MS in physics or a related �ield plus teaching experience. A \nPhD in physics and experience teaching undergraduate physics are preferred. \n \nInterested candidates should submit a CV and contact information for three references to \nDr. Peter Cha at pcha@adrian.edu. Questions about the position should be directed to Dr. \nCha. \n \nAdrian College is an Equal Opportunity Employer. "
    },
    "https://www.adrian.edu/files/assets/interiordesignadjunct-july2023.pdf": {
      "title": "Interior Design Adjunct",
      "description": "Interior Design Adjunct.  \n \nPosition Title:   Adjunct Instructor of Interior Design, Department of Art and Design  \nTeaching Load:          Part-time; up to 9 credit hours during the Fall 2023 Semester \nReporting Relationship: This position reports to the Chair of the Department and the Assistant Dean of Academic \nAffairs \nResponsibilities:   \nThis position involves frequent verbal, written and email correspondence with students and department faculty members. \nThe successful candidate will: \n•Instruct (In person) undergraduate courses in a variety of topics pertaining to Interior Design \n•Administer exams/projects and assign work outside the classroom \n•Report mid-term and final exam grades within College-specified timelines \n•Maintain regular availability to support students \nMinimum requirements: \n•Master’s Degree required in Graphic Design or Architecture,  \n•Demonstrated ability to foster positive interactions with diverse populations; \n•Must be able to perform duties within the normal bounds of a classroom environment.  \nDesired Qualifications:   \n•Experience teaching at the college level \n•Proficiency in software such as AutoCAD, Revit, and Enscape \nInterested individuals should submit a CV , unofficial transcripts, and contact information for three references to \nTravis Erxleben, (terxleben@adrian.edu). \nApplications will be accepted and reviewed until the position is filled \nAdrian College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. If offered a position, finalist must furnish proof of U.S. \ncitizenship or proof of eligibility to work in the U.S. within three (3) days of commencing employment.  \nA private, co-educational college of liberal arts and sciences related to the United Methodist Church, Adrian College is a \ntraditional four-year residential institution offering a focused undergraduate and graduate education. The College offers a \nmultitude of majors and pre-professional programs including certifications, approximately 50 athletic teams and 10 \ninstitutes dedicated to a diverse and personalized approach to education. Adrian College features state-of-the-art facilities, \nsmall class sizes and an innovative approach to the academic and personal growth of its students.  \nU.S. News & World Report has recognized and ranked Adrian College as a ‘Best Regional Colleges for Undergraduate \nTeaching’ and ‘Most Innovative’. Adrian also received accolades as a “Best Value” school. The College landed on the list \nof Best Value schools in the Midwest region for the sixth consecutive year and has also been recognized as having ‘A \nFocus on Student Success’; a new section in college rankings. Adrian College is located in Southeast Michigan, about 45 \nminutes from Ann Arbor and Toledo. \n"
    },
    "https://www.adrian.edu/files/assets/adjunctprofessorofmathematics-june2025.pdf": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor of Mathematics",
      "description": "Adjunct Professor of Mathematics.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \nPosition Title: Adjunct Professor of Mathematics \nTeaching Load: Part-time; 8 credit hours during the Fall 2025 Semester \n \nContext: \nThe Adrian College Mathematics Department announces an adjunct instructor position beginning Fall 2025. \nThe initial appointment will be for one semester, renewable based on performance. The department seeks a \ncandidate with a strong desire to teach and an eagerness to work with undergraduate students. \n \nResponsibilities:   \nThe main responsibility is to teach two sections of Math 115, Precalculus, for a total of 8 credit hours. The \nsuccessful candidate will also be required to: \n• Assign work outside the classroom, included but not limited to WebAssign. \n• Report mid-term and final grades within college-specified timelines. \n• Maintain regular availability to support students. \n \nMinimum requirements: \n• Candidates must have earned a Master degree or higher in Mathematics, Applied Mathematics, or \nStatistics by August 2025. \n• Have demonstrated ability to foster positive interactions with diverse populations. \n• Must be able to perform duties within the normal bounds of a college classroom environment. \n \nDesired Qualifications:   \n• Experience teaching mathematics at the college level. \n• Proficiency using course management software such as BlackBoard. \n \n \nInterested individuals should submit a CV, unofficial transcripts, statement of teaching philosophy, and \ncontact information for three references to the Math Search committee (math_search_2025@adrian.edu). \n \n \nApplications will be accepted and reviewed until the position is filled \n \nAdrian College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. If offered a position, finalist must furnish \nproof of U.S. citizenship or proof of eligibility to work in the U.S. within three (3) days of commencing \nemployment. \n"
    },
    "https://www.adrian.edu/files/assets/1-spanishadjunct.pdf": {
      "title": "Spanish Adjunct",
      "description": "Spanish Adjunct. ____________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________ \n \nPosition Title: Adjunct Instructor of Spanish, Department of Humanities \n \nWork Schedule: Part-time; 3-4 credit hours during the Spring 2026 Semester \n \nExample of Duties: \n \nThe Adrian College Department of Humanities announces an adjunct instructor position for Spring 2026 \nto teach upper-level Spanish courses. The successful candidate will foster communicative competence in \nspeaking, listening, reading, writing, and Hispanic cultures. The successful candidate will: \n \n• Teach one upper-level Spanish course (specific course assignment based on departmental need). \n• Design and deliver engaging instruction that strengthens advanced language proficiency.  \n• Incorporate authentic materials from Spanish-speaking regions, including literature, film, and media. \n• Develop assignments that encourage research, discussion, and written expression in Spanish \n• Assess student progress and provide timely, constructive feedback. \n• Maintain regular availability to support students outside of scheduled class time. \n• Collaborate with faculty in the Humanities to ensure course alignment with departmental goals and \nlearning outcomes. \n• Follow all other Adrian College policies related to duties.  \n \nMinimum Requirements: \n \n• Master’s degree in Spanish, Hispanic Studies, Applied Linguistics, or a closely related field by \nJanuary 2026. \n• Native or near-native proficiency in Spanish and English. \n• Demonstrated success teaching advanced-level Spanish courses at the college or university level. \n• Strong commitment to student-centered instruction and intercultural learning. \n• Ability to integrate technology and diverse media into classroom teaching. \n \nDesired Qualifications: \n \n• Ph.D. or ABD in Spanish, Hispanic Studies, Applied Linguistics, or a closely related field by January \n2026. \n• Experience teaching upper-level courses in areas such as Spanish literature, culture, translation, or \nSpanish for the professions. \n• Familiarity with course management software such as Blackboard and Blackbaud.  \n• Demonstrated ability to support inclusive and engaging learning environments. \n \nInterested individuals should submit a CV, unofficial transcripts, statement of teaching philosophy, \ncontact information for three references, and job application (AC Application) to the Dean of \nUndergraduate Studies, Dr. Nathan Gordon, at ngordon@adrian.edu. \n \nApplications will be accepted and reviewed until the position is filled \n \n\nAdrian College is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. If offered a position, the finalist must \nfurnish proof of U.S. citizenship or proof of eligibility to work in the U.S. within three (3) days of \ncommencing employment. \n \nA private, co-educational college of liberal arts and sciences related to the United Methodist Church, \nAdrian College is a traditional four-year residential institution offering a focused undergraduate and \ngraduate education. The College offers a multitude of majors and pre-professional programs including \ncertifications, approximately 50 athletic teams, and multiple institutes dedicated to a diverse and \npersonalized approach to education. Adrian College features state-of-the-art facilities, small class sizes \nand an innovative approach to the academic and personal growth of its students. \n \nU.S. News & World Report has recognized and ranked Adrian College as a ‘Best Regional College for \nUndergraduate Teaching’ and ‘Most Innovative’. Adrian also received accolades as a “Best Value” \nschool. The College landed on the list of Best Value schools in the Midwest region for multiple years \nrunning and has also been recognized as having ‘A Focus on Student Success’; a new section in college \nrankings. Adrian College is located in Southeast Michigan, about 45 minutes from Ann Arbor and \nToledo. \n "
    }
